#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a74d2eb-fe22-5c2d-b31f-9446f8f080d3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Overlapping Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "9cf4daed-1d80-5a74-90dc-b0b1da0ce6b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1695" :question/problem "Simplify the union of intervals $(-\\infty,2) \\cup [-1,3].$" :question/worked-solution "If we plot these two intervals on number lines, we get the following:\n\n![](images/27/2720cafe76aff8eb85ca65851570087505ee57a4b51904ee075aa86436cfc475.png)\n\n![](images/c3/c379dc0e0c371832a53e62da4505cfc0b03df50efc7241c0100794a191c8fb97.png)\n\nThe union of the two intervals consists of all the points which are contained within at least one of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/c4/c43257d8d30711804848039b3b8458302e0030e7512fb7cbf1f4b697263e5155.png)\n\nThis graph represents the interval $(-\\infty,3].$ So, the union $(-\\infty,2) \\cup [-1,3]$ simplifies to the interval $(-\\infty,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6619614-04df-5a22-afa8-77a41b016330" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Overlapping Intervals Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "489ef834-7015-5592-a5f5-b0daa3bc9f7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6278" :question/problem "What is the union of the intervals shown on the number lines below?\n\n![](images/60/603da954fd0e711ffa4879c51db847095edd9b1f256bbef66ad9de91c8cb31b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have the following number lines:\n\n![](images/60/603da954fd0e711ffa4879c51db847095edd9b1f256bbef66ad9de91c8cb31b4.png)\n\nThe union of the two intervals consists of all the points which are contained within at least one of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/58/5829d80a75446161fba99c08b361083a025a0c341fa41cf0608867d220dfa538.png)\n\nThe graph represents the interval $(-1,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068666 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eee95dd4-7d0c-5c18-bcb0-9b033e0cb509" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Inequality as a Union of Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "d0e51ce4-7f83-545e-9c3c-b79bf58d0eab" :question/math-academy-id "e-952" :question/problem "Express the compound inequality $x < 4$ or $x \\ge 10$ using interval notation." :question/worked-solution "We can represent the compound inequality on a number line, as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26594fb87ed7eca0803d554e4a1fbd3d3719cc01ba4e70f96126b73924ac5769.png)\n\nThis number line consists of the interval $(-\\infty, 4)$ as well as the interval $[10, \\infty).$\n\nSo, we can express the compound inequality as the union of these two intervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 4) \\:{\\cup}\\: [10, \\infty)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068667 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1300ab0b-313f-5489-a836-79cf200082f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Non-Overlapping Intervals as an Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "c575830c-0100-5cec-a3f8-70e29cba3b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-954" :question/problem "Express $\\left(-\\infty, -1 \\right) \\cup [2, \\infty)$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](images/eb/ebc7db3faf2d9eafb887e93e9cf3613853914bf02ef87b6f63b638494c55a2ac.png)\n\nThe interval $\\left(-\\infty, -1 \\right)$ can be expressed as the inequality $x < -1.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $[2, \\infty)$ can be expressed as the inequality $x \\geq 2.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx < -1 \\text{ or } x \\geq 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1300ab0b-313f-5489-a836-79cf200082f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Non-Overlapping Intervals as an Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b52c9ee-bfd5-5b5f-ab04-aa9353b48bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\sqrt{2}$ or $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505184} {:answer/id #uuid "45a7ca98-fb26-51e1-9c49-a9036f1c4db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}<x\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505247} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5525d2-9f39-5e3c-bbcc-23f7c21ab500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}\\le x<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505353} {:answer/id #uuid "7f940c01-971d-5e05-8515-5368e57d4c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\sqrt{2}<x$ or $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505360} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb6b56c-e9a5-5054-ba80-92bf42db3739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\sqrt{2}$ or $-1>x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b52c9ee-bfd5-5b5f-ab04-aa9353b48bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -\\sqrt{2}$ or $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505184} :answer-field/id #uuid "35dd5252-ecb4-5ab7-9fee-887856b84d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23a12ef5-c982-5f9f-a9b3-040c1ca03ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-102117" :question/problem "Express $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2}]\\cup (-1,\\infty )$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](images/65/65f772a179911c50e8159f2c24a2bfcc4a73ea00312ac84ced35627c635bc943.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2}]$ can be expressed as the inequality $x\\le -\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $(-1,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x>-1.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx\\le -\\sqrt{2}\\text{ or }x>-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1300ab0b-313f-5489-a836-79cf200082f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Non-Overlapping Intervals as an Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122e9de8-8fd7-5239-b4e9-4f05dd907ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\le x\\le 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505134} {:answer/id #uuid "150f838c-391a-5a27-8ded-eab8714b53ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<3$ or $x>6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505144} {:answer/id #uuid "21d3cac3-664e-5eec-a4a9-f1348f8d813a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3<x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505162} {:answer/id #uuid "8901d5b8-a296-5fa5-b047-8807d0b5213d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 3$ or $x\\ge 6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505371} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bb820a-dece-5f31-b89f-bb561b74daa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\le x$ or $6\\ge x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8901d5b8-a296-5fa5-b047-8807d0b5213d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 3$ or $x\\ge 6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505371} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f6c658-ad82-584e-82da-5182b08333d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c44a64a8-cdbe-51ac-84cd-5e8ba651f598" :question/math-academy-id "q-102131" :question/problem "Express $(-\\infty ,3]\\cup [6,\\infty )$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the two intervals on a number line:\n\n![](images/ec/ec4128763f85e601def705f1133bcff5cd7956cdcc10bfe438bd094a127c928a.png)\n\nThe interval $(-\\infty ,3]$ can be expressed as the inequality $x\\le 3.$\n\nLikewise, the interval $[6,\\infty )$ can be expressed as the inequality $x\\ge 6.$\n\nSo, the union of these intervals represents the compound inequality\n\n$$\nx\\le 3\\text{ or }x\\ge 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6619614-04df-5a22-afa8-77a41b016330" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Overlapping Intervals Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9c9050-d909-5ae5-bd9e-34465964c574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505224} {:answer/id #uuid "46c624a8-bf1f-5a3a-93ba-103d95597722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[2,6]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505252} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a56ce-0b87-5ed7-92fb-c1a82c75308a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505291} {:answer/id #uuid "77a5ded1-62f5-5782-8d4b-ec2b9d716610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,2]"