e of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/32/32899816340ae23edc2fdca86e13a1d04e04af1e5723beda1a684c6e51e16f4d.png)\n\nThe corresponding inequality is $x\\le 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a74d2eb-fe22-5c2d-b31f-9446f8f080d3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Union of Overlapping Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e09907c-0df3-5e21-b82b-3717bb2a5292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505129} {:answer/id #uuid "230152a8-6db6-50c0-937d-80a5af5ef9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505164} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc4fbfd-c5e5-5d76-9f4a-c68404a49197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505264} {:answer/id #uuid "79d429f3-c8df-53ff-9bbb-a4208f70a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505351} {:answer/id #uuid "89fbc493-05eb-565e-977c-e66a3553922a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79d429f3-c8df-53ff-9bbb-a4208f70a478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505351} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f453e2-8714-5191-b9f1-9eb4e9ebf046" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4302277-24d7-598f-a06e-35350706f2d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-59475" :question/problem "Simplify the union of intervals $(-2,3]\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "If we plot these two intervals on number lines, we get the following:\n\n![](images/05/05b3e2dc395b905a8b38a0827a3cf910e019d162e1313f9d96f6a8b5f2b39bbe.png)\n\n![](images/0f/0f4b29602999be5b6a51b6cc473b835cea6ccd39ea0aab441a48ac043cd2129c.png)\n\nThe union of the two intervals consists of all the points which are contained within at least one of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/cc/cc2ee8b7b308b6bcb75ea3ed2a4f3aee6018fb54771fadc4713e188525a2db44.png)\n\nThe graph represents the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the union $(-2,3]\\cup (1,\\infty )$ simplifies to the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eee95dd4-7d0c-5c18-bcb0-9b033e0cb509" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing an Inequality as a Union of Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1431a0b8-bddc-58fa-a59c-29d97f15f124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]\\cup [\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505143} {:answer/id #uuid "54b2169a-67d2-57ac-8ff7-5f9eeb8a24b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup [\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505273} {:answer/id #uuid "bf90322d-d809-5362-acaa-46933145dcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})\\cup [1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505478} {:answer/id #uuid "e2bdd80f-67d7-58ae-98cf-2afb442d613f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]\\cup (\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505546} {:answer/id #uuid "fc29ff11-e790-56a2-b43a-344cc6dc13f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b2169a-67d2-57ac-8ff7-5f9eeb8a24b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)\\cup [\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505273} :answer-field/id #uuid "c360023c-1f74-55ab-b7d9-98c50efbcfd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fa36828-f19c-51ba-9a63-f847dba2389e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75745" :question/problem "Express the compound inequality $x<1$ or $x\\ge \\sqrt{2}$ using interval notation." :question/worked-solution "We can represent the compound inequality on a number line, as follows:\n\n![](images/e5/e57c442c1524bf9c62feaefb1215cdfde23fee7dbbaf4207ce2e62bc8655769b.png)\n\nThe number line consists of the interval $(-\\infty ,1)$ as well as the interval $[\\sqrt{2},\\infty ).$\n\nSo, we can express the compound inequality as the union of these two intervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,1)\\cup [\\sqrt{2},\\infty )\n$$"}]}
